# `armbfiwidth` -- FIX DESIGN: the third site of an idiom an earlier round fixed at two sites and missed here

Nonce: CODEX_ARMBFI_20260910.  Open with `VERDICT:` naming the fix shape and the rows, then echo the nonce.

Ordinary C-correctness and instruction-decode work on an ARM CPU model.  **Please do not simply ratify --
check the premises.**  **The tree is `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\src\`**; three unrelated
2021-vintage copies of this project sit elsewhere under the project root, so line numbers off by hundreds
mean you are in one of those.  **Enumerate arithmetic rather than reasoning about it.**  **Verdict first.**

## §C0 -- state
HEAD `4eac0b1`.  Three unrelated rounds are held in the working tree (`dev_lca.c`, `dev_sgi_re.c`,
`cpu_alpha_palcode.c`); **none touches ARM**, so `cpu_arm_instr.c` is clean at HEAD and this round is
independent of all three.  This row has never had a fix design panel.

## §C -- SETTLED PREMISES (check them; say which you cannot settle from the packet)
S1. THE SITE: `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c`, the `X(bfi)` handler (Bit-Field Insert):
    ```c
    int lsb = (uint8_t)ic->arg[2];
    int msb = ic->arg[2] >> 16;
    int width = msb - lsb + 1;
    x <<= lsb;
    uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
    ```
S2. THE DECODE gives `msb` and `lsb` INDEPENDENTLY, each 0..31, at the decoder arm matched by
    `(iword & 0x0fe00070) == 0x07c00010`:
    `int lsb = (iword >> 7) & 31; int msb = (iword >> 16) & 31; ic->arg[2] = (msb << 16) + lsb;`
    So `width = msb - lsb + 1` ranges over **[-30, 32]**.
S3. `1 << width` is a SIGNED shift of `int` 1.  It is well defined only for `width` in [0, 30] here;
    `width` of 32 or more, and every negative `width`, is undefined in C.  The row's filed claim is that
    **468 of the 1024 encodable (msb, lsb) pairs reach an undefined shift**, and that **465 of those 468
    also have architecturally invalid field ordering (msb < lsb)** -- leaving a small set that is
    architecturally VALID and still reaches the undefined shift.  **That arithmetic is a premise: walk all
    1024 pairs and give me the true counts.**
S4. **AN EARLIER ROUND, `#510` (armbfxwidth32), FIXED THIS EXACT IDIOM AT TWO SITES AND LEFT THIS ONE.**
    It repaired `X(ubfx)` and `X(sbfx)`.  Read what it did (`git log --oneline --all | grep -i armbfx`,
    and the `#510` block in `CHANGELOG.md`) and tell me the SHAPE it chose, because this round should
    follow the house precedent rather than invent a third form -- unless the precedent is wrong here, in
    which case say so.  The filed row's own words: *"`#510` fixed TWO of THREE sites of one idiom and left
    the third, whose undefined domain is WIDER than either it repaired."*
S5. THE MANUAL IS NOW LOCAL and is the right one: `_scratchpad/ddi0406.txt` (ARM ARM, ARMv7-A/R).  The
    OLDER `ddi0100i.txt` PREDATES ARMv6T2 and contains no BFI/BFC/UBFX/SBFX at all, which is why ARM
    bitfield rows were once blocked for want of a source that was simply the wrong manual.  `:18066`
    (A8.8.20, BFI) reads: `if msbit >= lsbit then R[d]<msbit:lsbit> = R[n]<(msbit-lsbit):0>; // Other bits
    of R[d] are unchanged  else UNPREDICTABLE;`.  **Quote what you actually read; do not take my quote.**
S6. A VEHICLE EXISTS: `regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py` already executes ARM instructions on an unmodified
    in-tree machine and was built for `#510`.  So the cost here is adding rows, not building a harness.

## THE QUESTIONS
1. **PREMISES AS QUESTIONS.**  Walk all 1024 (msb, lsb) pairs and give the true counts for S3.  Separately:
   which pairs are architecturally VALID (`msb >= lsb`) and STILL reach a shift C does not define?  That
   subset is the part a fix must get right for reasons other than tidiness, and I want its exact size and
   an example.
2. **THE FIX SHAPE.**  What does `#510` do at the two repaired sites, verbatim?  Should this site copy it
   exactly?  If the architecture says the invalid-ordering case is UNPREDICTABLE with no required
   destination value, then preserving the destination is an emulator POLICY choice and may NOT be described
   as architecture -- so say what the fix should DO for `msb < lsb`, and what the comment may claim.
3. **THE SMALLEST EDIT THAT BREAKS YOUR FIX AND STILL PASSES** a naive row set.  Name the row that must
   exist to catch it.  In particular consider a fix that handles the negative-width case and leaves
   `width == 32` (`msb == 31, lsb == 0`) still shifting by 32.
4. **THE ROWS.**  Given the existing probe, what rows does this round owe?  For each: the instruction word
   (or how to build it), the register seeding, the expected result, and which variant it catches.  Say
   which rows would be VACUOUS -- passing identically on the fixed build and on a build with the defect --
   because on many hosts the undefined shift happens to produce the value the fix produces, and a row that
   cannot tell those apart is worse than no row.  **That is my main worry about this round.**
5. **Is there a FOURTH site of this idiom?**  `#510` found three.  Grep the tree for the pattern -- a shift
   whose count is a computed width -- and tell me whether any remain, in ARM or elsewhere.  A round that
   fixes the third of three and misses a fourth repeats the exact failure being repaired.
6. If you found something else while checking, say so.

