Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Mon Sep 7 19:56:13 2026 -0500

    #510 armbfxwidth32: UBFX and SBFX built their mask with an undefined shift
    
    cpu_arm_instr.c.  The decoder gives both handlers `width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)',
    so width reaches 32 -- and `1' is a plain int, so `(1 << width) - 1' was undefined at
    BOTH ends: a shift count equal to the type's width at 32 (C99 6.5.7p3) and a signed
    overflow at 31 (6.5.7p4).  `sbfx' had a SECOND undefined shift, `1 << (width-1)',
    with the `width < 32' test that would have made it moot on the NEXT line.
    
    Three sites:
    
        uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
        if (width < 32 && (x & (1U << (width - 1))))
                x |= ~mask;
    
    ONLY THE WIDTH-32 CASE IS OBSERVABLE, and that is measured: `UBFX r0,r1,#0,#32' of
    0x89abcdef returned 0x0.  At 31 the expression is undefined TWICE and wraps back to
    the right answer, so `1U' is owed by the language and buys no measurable change.
    The severity sentence says so rather than claiming both widths were broken.
    
    WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified `-E testarm':  regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py
    
        HEAD  9/11    FIX 11/11    FIX 12/12 after pass 2 added `s clear'
    
    `pc adv = 0x8004' on every row is the load-bearing control: the width-32 symptom is
    EXACTLY ZERO, indistinguishable from a mis-encoded word that never ran.  The
    encodings derive from the decoder, and the source register is `rm', not `rn' --
    `rn's bits 19:16 are occupied by widthm1.
    
    PASS 2 RATIFIED THE REPAIR AND REFUSED THE COMMENTS.  grok found three one-line
    escapes that score full marks.  The third -- deleting the bit test so width < 32
    always sign-extends -- is closed in-round by `s clear' (SBFX #1,#4 gives field 0x7,
    bit 3 CLEAR; every BYTE field of 0x89abcdef has bit 7 set, which is why nothing saw
    it).  The other two CANNOT be closed by any row: `1U'->`1' and restoring topBitMask
    are DEFINEDNESS repairs, and definedness has no guest-visible value on a
    non-trapping host.  Disclosed in the probe header, and `s w32' is relabelled for
    what it actually pins -- the MASK, not topBitMask and not the hoist.
    
    *** UBSAN COULD PIN THEM AND THIS ROUND DOES NOT USE IT. ***  build-asan carries
    -fsanitize=address,undefined.  A differential closes the whole class -- eight sites
    across three CPUs, none visible to a value oracle.  But that is a second binary and
    a STDERR oracle, i.e. a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE, and this round's pass 2 had already
    run.  A lane added after a pass is a lane nobody reviewed.  Filed as ubsanshiftlane
    and raised as fable_queue 7h-1, because DISCLOSE was the cheaper disposition and
    that is exactly when it wants adjudication rather than my say-so.
    
    ONE CLAIMED BREACH REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT: glm said deleting `x |= ~mask' still
    scores 11/11.  Built with a pre-registered prediction; measured 10/11, killed by
    `s mid' alone.
    
    CORRECTED IN PLACE: five comment overclaims (the mask/INT_MIN sentences describe
    this host's lowering, not C99 values; the sbfx block was a verbatim copy quoting a
    UBFX measurement; the && reorder is a UB improvement rather than a mere reorder, and
    with `1U' the hoist is redundant; "no sign to extend" was the wrong reason), and the
    496-count -- 465 pairs are strictly negative, 31 give width 0, and 496 is the
    msb < lsb population.  glm and grok caught that independently.
    
    SCOPE.  bfi, the two is_userpage sites, m88k_rot and MIPS X(ext) are excluded, each
    with its reason in the diff.  The kimi substitute challenged the bfi rationale as
    too broad -- its msb=31/lsb=0 cell is architecturally DEFINED and is the same
    defect -- and that challenge is carried to fable_queue 7h-2 rather than answered
    here.  Both stages are held at 7/9 awaiting opus5 and fable5.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

