1219:# `#510` armbfxwidth32: UBFX and SBFX built their mask with an undefined shift
1220:
1221:**THE DEFECT.** `cpu_arm_instr.c`. The decoder gives both handlers
1222:`width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` (`:4486`, `:4493`), so **`width` reaches 32** �?" and `1` is a
1223:plain `int`:
1224:
1225:    :1316  ubfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1226:    :1340  sbfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1227:    :1344  sbfx   uint32_t topBitMask = 1 << (width-1);
1228:
1229:`1 << 32` shifts by the full width of the type (C99 6.5.7p3); `1 << 31` overflows a signed `int`
1230:(6.5.7p4). Both are undefined. `UBFX rd, rn, #0, #32` is a legal A32 encoding �?" the architectural
1231:constraint is `lsb + width <= 32`, which `lsb = 0, width = 32` satisfies �?" so this is
1232:guest-reachable, not a decoder curiosity.
1233:
1234:> ### *** ONLY THE WIDTH-32 CASE IS OBSERVABLE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. ***
1235:>
1236:> Three pass-1 seats said the defect is "two widths wide". That is right about the LANGUAGE and
1237:> needs a qualifier about the SYMPTOM. With `r1 = 0x89abcdef`:
1238:>
1239:>     width  correct       on the committed build   observable?
1240:>     32     0x89abcdef    0x00000000               YES -- the whole register becomes zero
1241:>     31     0x09abcdef    0x09abcdef               no  -- same answer
1242:>
1243:> **At 31 the expression is undefined TWICE and wraps back to the right answer:** `1 << 31` gives
1244:> `INT_MIN`, and `INT_MIN - 1` gives `INT_MAX` = `0x7fffffff`, which IS the correct 31-bit mask.
1245:> **The repair is owed by the language at both widths; only one of them was returning a wrong
1246:> value**, and the round's severity sentence says so rather than claiming both.
1247:
1248:**THE FIX**, at all three sites:
1249:
1250:    uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
1251:
1252:    if (width < 32 && (x & (1U << (width - 1))))       /*  sbfx, hoisted above the shift  */
1253:            x |= ~mask;
1254:
1255:**`1U` is load-bearing and not style: it is what defends width 31; the guard defends 32.** That
1256:distinction was itself a correction �?" the round's own brief claimed the m88k idiom is safe because
1257:of its GUARD, and three seats pointed out the CAST is what covers `w == 31` there.
1258:
1259:At `sbfx`, the `width < 32` test that would have made `topBitMask` moot **sat on the line after
1260:the shift that needed it**. Reordering the `&&` is semantically identical for every width in
1261:`{1..32}` �?" both operands are ordinary locals with no side effects �?" and short-circuits before
1262:the shift.
1263:
1264:**THE FORK'S OWN IDIOM**, found by running the grep-the-fork step before writing the questions
1265:rather than after: `cpu_m88k_instr.c:532` and `:539`, in `X(clr)` and `X(set)` �?"
1266:`w == 0 ? 0xffffffff : ((uint32_t)1 << w) - 1`, where the m88k encoding spells full width as `0`.
1267:
1268:**WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified `-E testarm`:** `regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py`. A cold guest
1269:plants one instruction word with `put w`, sets `pc`, steps once, and reads `r0` and `pc` back.
1270:
1271:    HEAD  9/11   witness_HEAD_20260907.raw       FIX  11/11  witness_FIXED_20260907.raw
1272:                                                    FIX  14/14  witness_FIXED14_20260907.raw
