# *** THIS IS NOT THE SCRATCHPAD.  DO NOT READ STATE OUT OF THIS DIRECTORY. ***

The live scratchpad is the PROJECT-ROOT one, ONE LEVEL UP:

    C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\_scratchpad\

`tools/panel.sh` writes there and nowhere else -- its own line is
`OUT="${2:-$PROJ/_scratchpad/panel_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)}"`, and `$PROJ` is the PROJECT ROOT,
which is one level ABOVE this git repo.

## What this directory is, and why it is dangerous rather than merely untidy

It is a stale shadow copy.  On 2026-09-09 it held a `CHECKPOINT.md` four days old (with an EMPTY
`HEAD:` line, asserting `NOTHING IN FLIGHT` while `#516` was in flight) and **four panel
directories from 2026-09-08** -- `panel_tbi_20260908`, `panel_514p6_20260908`,
`panel_514p5_20260908`, `panel_bfi_20260908`.

**THE TRAP IS THE PATH, NOT THE CONTENT.**  Two standing instructions name `_scratchpad/`
RELATIVELY:

* the carrier's *"first stop each session: `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md`"*;
* the carrier's *"after firing, confirm a NEW `_scratchpad/panel_<timestamp>/` dir exists"*.

And the carrier separately records that the session cwd **DRIFTS into `GXEMUL-SEC/` for whole
rounds**.  From there both of those relative paths resolve HERE -- and this directory answers both
of them with yesterday's content, confidently and in the right shape.

## IT FIRED.  Measured 2026-09-09, twice in one session, and the second one is the instructive half

1. The stale `CHECKPOINT.md` was found and replaced with a redirect stub.  **That fix was
   incomplete, because it treated the file as the problem.**
2. Twenty minutes later, `ls -dt _scratchpad/panel_* | head -1` was run from `GXEMUL-SEC/` to read
   a panel that had JUST been fired.  It returned `panel_tbi_20260908` -- **yesterday's
   `alphatbinegsel` round** -- and six verdict lines were read out of it as though they were the
   new panel's.  The tell was semantic, not mechanical: the verdicts discussed a TBI/ASM guard,
   which is not what the brief asked about.  A command with a fallback (`[ -z "$P" ] && ...`)
   could not help, because the wrong answer was NON-EMPTY.

> **A DUPLICATE DIRECTORY IS WORSE THAN A MISSING ONE.**  A missing path errors.  This one returns
> a plausible, well-formed, WRONG answer -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape wearing a filesystem
> costume, and the same class as the three pristine-2021 `src/` copies the carrier warns about.

## THE RULE

**Never a bare `_scratchpad/`.**  Use the absolute path, or `$PROJ/_scratchpad/`, or
`GXEMUL-SEC/../_scratchpad/`.  When a panel is fired, confirm the dir by its **TIMESTAMP matching
the launch**, not by "newest match" -- and read one verdict line to confirm it is about the round
you just briefed.  *Semantic confirmation is what caught this; no path check would have.*

## Why this file is not simply deleted along with the directory

Deleting is the owner's call, not a round's, and the contents are somebody's working artifacts.
The stale files here are ALSO the evidence for the incident above.  `_scratchpad/` is untracked by
standing directive, so nothing here is in git, and this note costs nothing to keep.
