tools/pipeline/ledger.json:21494:   "state_note": "*** THIS ROW WAS FILED WITH THREE WRONG NUMBERS AND A CIRCULAR CITATION. The pass-2 adjudicator found all four; each is corrected here and the deriver is named so the next reader re-runs it instead of trusting me. ***\n\n**(1) THE CENSUS WAS WRONG FOR THE THIRD TIME, BY THE OPPOSITE ERROR TO THE SECOND.** Filed as 166/76/94. Correct, suffix-aware: **174 dirs / 78 cited / 96 uncited.** My first attempt used a loose `panel_*` glob that matched `.out` FILES (229/157); my 'correction' then required `panel_<8>_<6>` EXACTLY, which silently dropped **eight real dirs carrying suffixes** (`..._024101_audit`, `..._044509_ledger11`, `..._110328_0798b90_pass2` and five more). Too loose, then too tight. **And the structure already knew** -- `check_seats_read.py:74-77` documents the suffix reality and resolves bare stems to suffixed dirs. I wrote a filter instead of reading the one that works.\n\n**(2) THE ACTIONABLE SET IS EIGHT, NOT FOUR.** Four on 08-24, and FOUR MORE ON 08-25 that my exact-match filter could not see: `diffrec`, `ledger11`, `tailfacts`, and `0798b90_pass2`. **Two are heavyweight** (ledger11 carries a 1.29 MB codex answer; diffrec 1.07 MB) and **one is a PASS 2 ON A SHIPPED SOURCE-TOUCHING COMMIT** -- precisely the late-dissent shape where an unharvested answer reads as false unanimity.\n\n**(3) THE OVERSTATEMENT WAS ~12x, NOT ~4x.** 96 uncited against 8 actionable.\n\n*** (4) MY OWN NOTE MADE AN UNCITED DIR LOOK CITED, WHICH IS A DEFECT SHAPE I HAVE NOT SEEN BEFORE. *** The first draft listed that dir's name in full (prefix included), so a suffix-aware grep found it in the ledger and scored it CITED -- **on the strength of the sentence saying it was uncited.** A record ABOUT an unread review made the review look read. The adjudicator hit this too: its own first census scored that dir cited for the same reason.\n\n**RESOLVED BY STRIPPING THE `panel_` PREFIX in the backlog list below**, so the names stay human-readable and greppable-by-timestamp while NOT matching the citation pattern. *The adjudicator asked for the names in full; written in full they re-create the circularity, so this takes the instruction's intent -- make them findable -- by a form that cannot false-cite.*\n\nTHE BACKLOG (prefix stripped deliberately; prepend `panel_` to open them):\n    20260824_030624   5 substantial answers\n    20260824_034510   5\n    20260824_041606   7\n    20260824_124655   6\n    20260825_032831_diffrec     codex 1.07 MB, minimax 61 KB\n    20260825_044509_ledger11    codex 1.29 MB, deepseek 281 KB, seven seats\n    20260825_100232_tailfacts   MARGINAL -- two sub-2 KB answers; may close by annotation\n    20260825_110328_0798b90_pass2   *** pass 2 on a SHIPPED commit ***\n\n**THE CUTOFF IS RE-WARRANTED, because the reason I filed was an unchecked absence claim** -- the class this project mandates checking rather than asserting. I wrote 'older dirs belong to closed rounds whose findings WERE acted on' and checked none of them. The adjudicator checked THREE across two eras and found every finding ABSORBED: `20260822_184955`'s headline mutant is the shipped accept-row kill with its delivery rows in the gate; `20260823_140419`'s '(once per offset)' edit is in `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` verbatim at four sites; `20260813_183758`'s open question lives as the held `dfreq` row. **n=3, zero live misses** -- which supports refusing the 96-sweep on evidence instead of on convenience.\n\nSo the cutoff is not 'three days'. It is **2026-08-24, the citation rule's own adoption date, forward-only** -- the same dated-cutoff idiom as `HOLDQ_CUTOFF` and `check_bugfile_sync`'s `CUTOFF`. Older dirs stay under the read-side rule: re-derive before leaning, file anything found decayed. **Note the uncited population is NOT confined to the pre-ledger age** (08-22 has 12 of 17 uncited, 08-23 six of 12), so the cutoff is a policy line, not a natural one.\n\n**PRE-SPECIFIED, so the sample size is honest: a sample of three is not a proof. If the eight-dir harvest finds even ONE live unacted finding, sample the 08-22/23 band (18 dirs) before closing this row. If zero, close it and record the sample.**\n\n*** (5) AND THE ROW'S OWN FRAMING WAS OVERSTATED, REFUTED BY MY OWN COMMIT BODY. *** It said every unread-review instance that day was 'a finding sitting in a seat answer nobody had opened'. Commit `087a786` says of the largest instance, verbatim: *'not a review nobody read, but a review I read thoroughly and left unrecorded'*. **Today's instances are predominantly RECORDING gaps, not unopened findings.** The 'twice cost a full round to rediscover' clause is true of the HISTORICAL instances -- the kimi member-2 answer, `m8invpred` -- not of today's. That is the dropped-qualifier class: evidence for one case restated as a property of the day.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS VERY FIX WAS DEFEATED BY ITS OWN EXPLANATION. *** I stripped the `panel_` prefix from the backlog list, then wrote the full name in the sentence EXPLAINING the circularity -- so the dir was still scored cited. That is the correction-quotes-what-it-refutes hazard, documented in the carrier this same morning as the reason a naive stale-text grep *gets steadily more wrong as the file gets more honest*. **Here it defeated a fix inside the paragraph describing the fix.** The rule that follows is narrow and mechanical: **when a record must NAME an artifact it is asserting is UNRECORDED, the name must not be written in the form the checker matches -- anywhere in the record, including the prose about it.**\n\n**SEVERITY SET MED, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: EIGHT uncited panel dirs hold seat answers section G has never seen.  RAISED on 2026-09-07 evidence: five unread reviews were mined that day and one contained a LIVE defect (armbfiwidth) that #510 had missed.  This is no longer a hypothetical cost.",
