tools/pipeline/ledger.json:33118:   "state_note": "Derived 2026-09-07 while trying to obey the standing directive *prioritise critical bugs and also\neasy resolvable ones*.  **The directive is unexecutable over half the queue.**\n\n    open + in-work        187\n    carrying `sev`        100   (high 10, med 40, low 50)\n    UNRATED                87   = 47%\n    unrated AND WITNESSED  73\n\n**The 73 is the number that matters.**  A witness makes a row ELIGIBLE under the ladder, so those are\nrows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  Ranking is what the carrier says orders\nthe queue -- *rung is a floor on admissibility, never a priority; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order\nthe queue* -- so an unranked witnessed row is invisible to the only ordering rule this project has.\n\n*** THE FIX IS NOT A REGEX, AND I NEARLY SHIPPED ONE. ***  A keyword scan over titles buckets 7 rows\nunder host-kill language, 5 under memory-safety and 17 under wrong-data.  **Those buckets are\nCORRELATES.**  What makes a row HIGH here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no\nkeyword tests reachability -- which is the deriver rule (*key on what DEFINES the class, not on a\ncorrelate of it*) applied to my own triage script.  The scan is kept only as a READING ORDER.\n\n**Candidates the scan surfaced, recorded as UNVERIFIED reading order and explicitly NOT as gradings:**\n`gdromcondavail` (COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread), `asicindexcase` (a deleted\nguard that was also a bounds check), `gdrompiooddlen` (guards one byte, reads two), `asickillsurvive`\n(four kill rows survive-only, so an OOB read is unobserved), `gdromdmawidth` (a tail store ignoring\n`len`), `sh4pcic15` (an interrupt line that can never be delivered), `asicpermute` (an index\npermutation).  **Each needs its reachability read before any `sev` is written.**\n\n> **DISPOSITION: a RATING pass, evidence-first, one row at a time, with the reachability sentence\n> quoted from the source.**  It is read-only, so it parallelises freely and belongs to the file-reading\n> seats -- the exact work the standing directive says to give codex.  It is NOT a round: nothing is\n> edited, and the deliverable is `sev` plus a one-line warrant per row.\n\n*Related but distinct from [[baretitle]], which counts rows with an EMPTY NOTE.  A row can be richly\ndocumented and still unranked -- most of these 73 are.*\n\n*** MY '73 WITNESSED' FIGURE IS WRONG, AND IT IS THE CORRELATE TRAP FOR THE THIRD TIME TODAY. ***\ncodex: *\"**37 of those 73 have witness strings beginning `none`** -- sometimes followed by\nhistorical measurements, sometimes explicitly denying reachability.  **The snippet counts strings,\nnot demonstrated witnesses.**\"*\n\nMy test was `r.get('witness') and r.get('witness') != 'none'` -- which passes any string that merely\nSTARTS with `none`, and this ledger's convention is to write `none -- <why>` with the reasoning\nattached.  **So the honest figure is about 36 witnessed-and-unrated rows, not 73.**\n\n*The paragraph above this one warns against keying on a correlate, and the snippet printed\nimmediately below it does exactly that.  Re-derive with a prefix test, and note that `none -- ...`\nrows are often the BEST documented ones, since a denial of reachability takes more evidence than an\nassertion of it.*\n\n*** A PATTERN IN MY OWN METHOD, WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT IS NOW FOUR FOR FOUR TODAY: EVERY\nCENSUS I RAN KEYED ON ONE SPELLING AND MISSED A VARIANT. ***\n\n    severity          the ledger's field is `sev`              -> 0 of 260 rows matched\n    witness != 'none' the convention is `none -- <why>`        -> 37 of 73 miscounted as witnessed\n    << 24             uint16_t << 16 is the same defect        -> the structural site missed entirely\n    (int)idata        the tree also writes `(int) idata`       -> 226 where the answer is 309\n\n**Each was found by someone else running a different derivation, never by me re-reading mine.**  The\ncarrier's rule says *when two derivers disagree, neither is trustworthy until you look at what each\nMATCHED* -- but all four of these had only ONE deriver, so there was nothing to disagree with, and a\nsingle method produces a confident number with no signal attached.\n\n> **THE PRACTICE THAT FOLLOWS: a census that will be written into a durable record runs TWO\n> derivations with different keys, and prints BOTH.**  Agreement is weak evidence (they can share a\n> blindness -- `sgirekills` measured that) but DISAGREEMENT is a free, immediate signal, and it is\n> the only one available before a seat reads the work.\n\n*** AND THE CLOSURE PASS CAUGHT A FIFTH COUNT ERROR OF MINE, IN THE BRIEF THAT ANNOUNCED THE OTHER\nFOUR. ***\n\nI stated \"note/witness cites a `#NNN` round: **40**\".  codex: *\"I cannot reproduce 40 from the\nstated fields.  `#\\s*\\d+` over top-level `note` and `witness` selects **51**; including `state_note`\nproduces **98 at HEAD / 104 in the working ledger**.  **The field selection needs to accompany that\ncount.**\"*  My KEY2 ran over `witness` plus `state_note[:400]` -- a truncation I never wrote down.\n\n**It also caught that my census MIXED SNAPSHOTS:** HEAD has 184 open/in-work rows, the working\n(uncommitted) ledger has 190.  Both contain the same fourteen commit-bearing candidates, so the\nKEY1 result stands -- but a count taken from a dirty tree and compared against HEAD is two\npopulations wearing one number.\n\n*The two-deriver practice adopted this morning would not have caught either: both are failures to\nstate the DOMAIN, not to vary the key.  **A count owes its field selection and its snapshot, not\njust its command.***\n\n*** ADDENDUM 2026-09-07: THE GAP IS NOT A CLOSED LEGACY BLOCK, SO THE ORDER OF THE FIX MATTERS. ***\n\nRe-derived from the other direction while filing a DUPLICATE of this row ([[ledgersevmissing]], now\nwithdrawn -- see it for the process lesson).  Counting rows that lack the `sev` KEY outright, rather\nthan rows that are unrated:\n\n    total ledger rows                       411\n    lacking a `sev` key entirely            261\n    of those, open or in work                78\n    by state: open 65, in work 13, held 72, closed 93, dropped 18\n\n**The set spans the whole history, not just the early rounds.**  It contains `d1`, `a4`, `n1`..`n6`\nfrom the first rounds -- and also `wdcdataoutdrain`, `gdromlatchcross`, `divratchetband` and others\nopened within the last fortnight.  So rows are STILL being created without the field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ROW'S DISPOSITION: the writer-side check lands BEFORE the rating pass, not\n> after it.**  A backfill alone fixes the past and not the cause, and the queue refills behind it.\n> The check is the `check_cited_artifacts` idiom -- forward-only from a cutoff, so a NEW row without\n> a rating fails and the existing ones warn.  That ordering is the only thing the duplicate\n> contributed, and it is why it was folded here instead of being left as a second row.\n\n*Also observed while sorting: a row with no `sev` is not sorted LAST -- depending on the tool it is\nomitted entirely or it raises KeyError and kills the sort.  Both happened.  That is worse than a\nlow ranking, because nothing reports that the row was skipped.*\n\n*** THREE SUBSTANTIVE codex ANSWERS SAT ON DISK FOR THIS ROW WITH **ZERO** LEDGER ENTRIES, AND ONE\nOF THEM CONTAINED A LIVE DEFECT NOBODY HAD SEEN.  Recorded 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe three (`codex_shiftub`, `codex_bitsbytes`, `codex_helpers`, all dated 2026-09-07) were verified\nby CONTENT and TAIL -- real answers, no wall text, no echo -- and cited nowhere.  **`codex_bitsbytes`\nnamed the `(1 << width) - 1` idiom in the ARM bit-field handlers.  Its UBFX/SBFX citations were\nSTALE (repaired by `#510` before it ran), but following the finding into the file found the THIRD\nsite, `X(bfi)`, still live with a WIDER undefined domain than either the round had fixed** -- now\nfiled as `armbfiwidth`.\n\n> **A STALE FINDING THAT LEADS TO A LIVE ONE IS NOT A WRONG ANSWER.**  It is the sibling grep the\n> round should have run itself, arriving a day late from a seat.  The carrier's mechanical rule 2\n> -- *when correcting a claim, grep for its siblings before calling it fixed* -- is the rule `#510`\n> missed, and it is recorded there with a prior instance of exactly this shape.\n\n*And the same batch corrected my own arithmetic in a way I then confirmed by brute force: 496\nnonpositive widths of 1024 pairs, not the figure I had supplied.*\n\n*** RE-DERIVED 2026-09-07, AND THE ROW'S OWN NUMBERS WERE WRONG -- WHICH IS THE DEFECT THIS ROW IS\nABOUT, COMMITTED BY THIS ROW. ***\n\n    claimed in the title    measured 2026-09-07\n    47% unrated             34%  (65 of 189 open rows)\n    73 unrated + witnessed  16\n\n**AND MY FIRST RE-MEASUREMENT WAS ALSO WRONG, IN THE WAY I HAD DOCUMENTED AN HOUR EARLIER.**  I\nfiltered `witness not in ('none', '')` and got **59** -- because the field holds prose like\n`none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable` and `n/a -- a harness gap`, which are not equal to\n`none` and are not witnesses either.  **That is the deriver rule's own failure: I keyed on a\ncorrelate (the string differs from a sentinel) instead of on what DEFINES the class (the field\nmatches the enforced schema).**  Keyed on `check_witness.py`'s own regex -- `^(boot:|probe:|\nconstruction:|harness:|detector:)` -- the answer is **16**.\n\n*Three counting methods, three answers (73, 59, 16), and only the one keyed to the enforced schema\nis defensible.  The tell was the same one this project keeps recording: when derivers disagree, look\nat what each MATCHED rather than choosing a number.*\n\n### *** THE CHARACTERISATION IS THE USEFUL PART, AND IT ANSWERS THE PRIORITISATION WORRY. ***\n\nOf the 16 unrated-and-witnessed rows, **14 carry `harness:` witnesses**, one `boot:` and one\n`detector:`.  **The unrated backlog is overwhelmingly TOOLING DEBT.**  So the rating gap is not\nconcealing critical emulator defects -- which is what the row's alarming headline implied and what a\nreader would reasonably fear.\n\n**All 16 were rated on 2026-09-07 by the MAIN LOOP** (2 of them raised on the day's own evidence;\nsee `panelcite0824`).  The remaining 49 unrated open rows carry NO witness of any kind, so they are\ncorrectly ranked below every witnessed row regardless of what severity they would eventually get --\n*a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE; severity orders what is already eligible.*"
