tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34394:     "hold_question": "`armbfiwidth` IS THE THIRD SITE OF AN IDIOM `#510` FIXED AT TWO, AND SIX SEATS AGREE ITS FORM DOES NOT TRANSFER -- so the question is not whether to fix it but WHAT THE ROUND MAY CLAIM.  SETTLED: UBFX/SBFX encode widthm1 so their width is never negative, while BFI encodes MSB so `width = msb - lsb + 1' reaches [-30, 32]; of 1024 encodable (msb,lsb) pairs, 468 reach an undefined shift and 465 of those also have architecturally invalid ordering.  The prescribed fix is `mask = (width <= 0) ? 0U : ((width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1))'.  AND THE ARM MANUAL IS NOW LOCAL AND READ: ddi0406.txt:18066 (A8.8.20) gives `if msbit >= lsbit ... else UNPREDICTABLE', with NO required destination value.  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) THE ROUND'S CLAIM: codex warns this is NOT definedness-only -- at width 32 the manual says BFI copies the whole register (`<width>' is 1 to 32-<lsb>), while under the count-masked host lowering `#510' describes, the OLD mask would be zero and leave the destination unchanged.  So a guest-visible BEHAVIOUR CORRECTION may be hiding inside what I would otherwise write up as a definedness repair, and NEITHER of us has measured that lowering at BFI.  Does the round owe that measurement before it may ship, or is an explicit disclosure enough?  (2) POLICY versus ARCHITECTURE: for `msbit < lsbit' the manual says UNPREDICTABLE and mandates nothing, so `mask = 0' (preserve the destination) is an EMULATOR POLICY choice.  Is silently preserving right, or does an UNPREDICTABLE encoding deserve a once-only announcement?  The tree has precedent for both and no universal policy.  (3) THE DETECTOR: `#510`'s own finding is that `1 << 31' and `1U << 31' give the SAME mask after the `- 1', so NO guest-value row can kill a `1U' -> `1' regression -- only UBSAN or a source pin can.  Given that, what may this round's detector honestly claim to cover, and does the disclosed `ubsanshiftlane' debt have to land first?",
