tools/pipeline/ledger.json-34533-   "commits": "",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34534:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 from the codex `armbfiwidth` design answer.  **BFC is BFI with Rn = 15**, and the architecture defines it as clearing the field rather than inserting from a register.  This fork's decoder does not distinguish them, so a BFC would insert bits taken from `r[15]` -- the PC -- instead of clearing.\n\n**NOT VERIFIED HERE.**  It comes from one seat's source reading, and neither the decoder arm's exact guard nor the architectural claim has been re-derived by me. *The round-scope rule keeps it separate from `armbfiwidth`: same file and adjacent lines, but a different defect with a different fix and a different oracle, so it is a candidate ROUND rather than a rider.*\n\n*** THE codex ASSESS ANSWER, 2026-09-08.  THE IMPLEMENTATION HALF IS VERIFIED; THE ARCHITECTURAL\nHALF IS NOT, AND THE SEAT SAID SO RATHER THAN FILLING THE GAP FROM MEMORY. ***\n\n### 1.  VERIFIED: source field 15 does reach the handler\n\nThe generic decoder computes `rm = iword & 15`, and the bfi arm binds `ic->arg[1] =\n(size_t)(&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm])` with **no guard excluding 15**.  `X(bfi)` then opens with\n`uint32_t x = reg(ic->arg[1]);` and has **no source-15 clear path**.  All three of my settled\npremises about the extraction and packing were confirmed against the file.\n\n### 2.  *** MY OWN CHARACTERISATION WAS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS SHARPER THAN THE CLAIM. ***\n\nI wrote that BFC becomes *\"BFI with Rn = PC\"*.  **`r[15]` is not the PC in this interpreter.**\nTranslation reconstructs the instruction address into `cpu->pc` WITHOUT refreshing `r[15]`, and the\nPC+8 that PC-relative loads need is computed into a separate `tmp_pc`.  So `r[15]` holds whatever\nwas last written there -- **a previous load-to-PC target, or an exception-vector address** -- which\nis a *stale* value, not a predictable one.\n\n*That matters for the fix and for any oracle: a row that assumes r[15] holds the current PC would\nbe pinning something the emulator does not maintain.*\n\n### 3.  *** AND MY MANUAL PREMISE WAS WRONG TOO -- THE PREMISE-AUDIT LINE EARNED ITS PLACE AGAIN. ***\n\nI told the seat DDI 0100I was ARMv5.  **Its preface explicitly includes ARMv6.**  It still does not\ncontain BFI or BFC -- the seat searched and found neither mnemonic -- because those are ARMv6T2\nadditions.\n\n> **SO THE ARCHITECTURAL CLAIM AT THE HEART OF THIS ROW REMAINS UNVERIFIED AGAINST ANY LOCAL\n> DOCUMENT, and the seat refused to paper over it:** *\"source 15 -> architectural BFC is not\n> document-verified within this reading scope; I cannot attach a local manual page citation to it.\"*\n> **That is the honest form this project asks for, and it is a BLOCKER: #357/#362/#365 shipped page\n> citations that came from model output rather than a local manual.**  This round does not ship a\n> behavioural change on a recalled architectural rule.  **It needs an ARMv7-A ARM (DDI 0406) in\n> `_scratchpad/`, and until then the row stays open with the implementation half verified.**\n\n### 4.  THE WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE, and the design turns on the r[15] correction above\n\nBecause `r[15]` is stale rather than the PC, the witness must PUT a known value there:\n\n    r0 = 0xffffffff\n    a word load into PC whose target is 0x00001008   -- this is what leaves 0x1008 in r[15]\n    at that target:  BFC r0, #8, #4                  -- lsb=8, msb=11, width=4\n\n    clear the field          -> r0 = 0xfffff0ff\n    insert the stored r[15]  -> r0 = 0xfffff8ff\n\n**Merely placing the instruction at 0x1008 does NOT establish that register value** -- the\nload-to-PC setup is what does it, which is precisely the consequence of finding 2.  Width 4 is\nchosen to avoid the sibling mask-boundary cases.  *Source-derived and arithmetic-checked; not run.*\n\n### 5.  TWO ROUNDS CONFIRMED, on the round-scope rule\n\n*\"Correcting source-15 semantics leaves mask construction unchanged; correcting mask construction\nleaves source-15 semantics unchanged.\"*  Different fix, different oracle -- so `armbfiwidth` stays\nseparate, as filed.\n\n*** THE MANUAL THIS ROW WAS BLOCKED ON IS NOW LOCAL, AND IT COVERS BFC TOO. ***\n`_scratchpad/ddi0406.txt` (ARM DDI 0406C.d), fetched 2026-09-08 at a seat's citation for the\nsibling row `armbfiwidth` and verified by reading.  **`:17995`, A8.8.19 BFC:**\n\n    if msbit >= lsbit then\n        R[d]<msbit:lsbit> = Replicate('0', msbit-lsbit+1);\n        // Other bits of R[d] are unchanged\n    else\n        UNPREDICTABLE;\n\nIdentical in structure to BFI's.  So this row no longer needs a source it does not have -- and the\nreason it appeared to need one is worth recording: **`ddi0100i.txt` is DDI 0100I, which predates\nARMv6T2 and contains no BFI, BFC, UBFX or SBFX at all.**  The block was the WRONG MANUAL, not a\nmissing one."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-34535-  },
