OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17147:### `armbfiwidth` (med) -- filed 2026-09-07
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17148:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17149:cpu_arm_instr.c X(bfi): `(1 << width) - 1' where width = msb - lsb + 1 ranges [-30,32] -- 468 of 1024 encodable (msb,lsb) pairs reach an undefined shift on a 32-bit-int host (a DIFFERENT 496 have architecturally invalid field ordering).  #510 fixed the SAME expression at UBFX and SBFX and left this third site fifty lines below, and its `== 32' form covers none of the negative widths
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17150:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17151:FILED 2026-09-07.  **`#510` fixed TWO of THREE sites of one idiom and left the third, whose undefined
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17152:domain is WIDER than either it repaired.**  Found by reading an UNREAD codex answer
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17153:(`regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_bitsbytes_20260907.txt`) that had been sitting on disk,
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17154:cited nowhere.  Every element below was VERIFIED by opening the file, not taken from the seat.
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17155:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17156:*** THE DEFECT. ***  `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c`, `X(bfi)` (Bit-Field Insert):
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17157:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17158:    int lsb = (uint8_t)ic->arg[2];
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17159:    int msb = ic->arg[2] >> 16;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17160:    int width = msb - lsb + 1;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17161:    x <<= lsb;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17162:    uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;        <-- UNDEFINED over most of its domain
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17163:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17164:**THE DECODE GIVES msb AND lsb INDEPENDENTLY, each 0..31** (verified at the `bfi` arm of the
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17165:decoder, `(iword & 0x0fe00070) == 0x07c00010`):
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17166:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17167:    int lsb = (iword >>  7) & 31;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17168:    int msb = (iword >> 16) & 31;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17169:    ic->arg[2] = (msb << 16) + lsb;
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17170:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17171:so **`width` ranges over [-30, 32]** and `1 << width` is undefined three separate ways:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17172:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17173:    width <= 0    negative shift count                       C99 6.5.7p3
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17174:    width == 32   count >= width of the promoted type        C99 6.5.7p3
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17175:    width == 31   1 is `int'; 2^31 is not representable       C99 6.5.7p4 (signed overflow)
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17176:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17177:*** THE DENOMINATOR, and it is nearly half. ***  Of the **1024** encodable (msb, lsb) pairs:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17178:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17179:    496   nonpositive width  (lsb >= msb + 1)   -- of which 465 are strictly negative
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17180:      1   width == 32        (msb=31, lsb=0)
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17181:      2   width == 31        (msb=lsb+30)
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17182:    ---
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17183:    499   of 1024 pairs reach an undefined shift
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17184:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17185:**That arithmetic was INDEPENDENTLY DERIVED TWICE and the first attempt was mine and wrong.**  A
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17186:codex answer on a different brief (`codex_shiftub_20260907.txt`) corrected it: *"`msb - lsb + 1 < 0`
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17187:requires `lsb >= msb + 2`, giving 465 negative widths.  Another 31 pairs produce zero; 496 is the
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17188:number of nonpositive widths."*  Recomputed here from the decode: pairs with `lsb >= msb+1` sum to
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17189:31+30+...+1 = **496**, and `lsb >= msb+2` sums to 30+29+...+1 = **465**.  *The seat was right.*
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17190:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17191:*** WHY THIS IS A SIBLING MISS AND NOT A NEW CLASS. ***  `#510` repaired the identical expression at
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17192:the UBFX and SBFX handlers, which now read:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17193:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17194:    uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17195:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17196:**`X(bfi)` sits about fifty lines below the second of those, in the same file, and was not touched.**
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17197:The carrier's mechanical rule 2 -- *when correcting a claim, grep for its siblings before calling it
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17198:fixed* -- is exactly the rule that was missed, and it is recorded there with a prior instance where
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17199:an "un-fakeable" claim was corrected in one place and survived in three.  **The BFX round's own
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17200:detector cannot see this**: `regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py` plants UBFX/SBFX words only.
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17201:
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17202:**AND THE BFX FIX DOES NOT TRANSFER UNCHANGED.**  BFX decodes `width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)`, so
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17203:its domain is {1..32} and a `width == 32` special case is sufficient.  **BFI's width can be zero or
OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:17204:negative, which no `== 32` test catches.**  A repair copied from the sibling would close one third of
